{"title":"Combining vector layers","markdown":{"headingText":"Combining vector layers","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-e04","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> **Dates of practical:** 23rd and 30th October 2025\n\nCreating spatial and thematic subsets of vector layers is essential GIS practice and a key step in preparing for the wind farm Project. Before you begin, familiarise yourself with the wind farm Project instructions.\n\nYou will find data for this exercise on the **L:** drive under `College of Science/Geography/GEG276`. Read alongside **GIS_guide_to_practical_exercises**.\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n## Learning aims\n\nClip small subsets from large vector datasets, create dissolved buffers around line and polygon layers, select subsets by attribute, and combine layers.\n:::\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Investigating your map tile\n\n(@) Launch QGIS, create a new project, and open layer `Wales_border` from `UK_Boundaries`.\n\n(@) From `Wales_map_tiles`, open the layer corresponding to your assigned OS map tile to see where your wind farm project will focus.\n\n(@) Calculate the area of your 20 × 20 km map tile (square metres) and its perimeter (metres).\n\n(@) Open the attribute table for your map tile. This layer has one polygon. Open the **Field Calculator** and explore `$area` and `$perimeter`. Values differ slightly from expectations due to Earth curvature and projection distortion.\n\n(@) Using prior methods, calculate the number of people living within your polygon at the 2021 Census. Compute population density for your tile.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Clipping polygon and point layers\n\n(@) Open layers in `Wales_protected_areas`. Investigate what “protected area” means and explore attribute tables. Save your project regularly.\n\n(@) Clip these layers to your map tile using **Vector → Geoprocessing Tools → Clip**. Input: the dataset to clip; Overlay: your map tile polygon. Choose **Save to File**, **GeoPackage** format, sensible names, and folder structure. Examine the new clipped layer.\n\n(@) Clip each protected area dataset relevant to your tile. Save clipped layers with appropriate filenames.\n\n(@) Merge protected area layers with **Vector → Data Management Tools → Merge Vector Layers** to create a single protected‑areas layer. Alternatives include **Union** or **MMQGIS → Merge**.\n\n(@) Dissolve internal boundaries with **Vector → Geoprocessing Tools → Dissolve**. Save to disk. Use the field calculator to find total protected area and percentage of your tile.\n\n(@) Using prior data, clip existing or planned wind farms to your map tile. Save your work.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Clipping line layers and adding buffers\n\n(@) From `British_Isles_geofabrik/wales`, open `waterways`, `water`, `railways`, and `roads`. Explore attributes.\n\n(@) Clip each of these layers to your tile. Name outputs sensibly.\n\n(@) Review wind farm Project constraints (e.g., distances from transport links and water bodies).\n\n(@) Create buffers with **Vector → Geoprocessing Tools → Buffer** for each transport and water layer. Dissolve boundaries (during or after) and optionally clip buffers to the tile polygon.\n\n(@) Create buffers for existing/planned wind farms if appropriate. Name layers carefully and organise files.\n\n(@) Merge buffered layers if helpful.\n\n(@) Consider merging clipped layers before buffering as an alternative workflow.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Selecting elements by attribute\n\n(@) Open `gis_osm_landuse_a_free_1` (landuse) and clip to your map tile.\n\n(@) Examine the attribute table. Open **Select by expression**, expand **Fields and Values**, choose `fclass`, and list unique values. Decide which classes are relevant (e.g., `residential`, `forest`).\n\n(@) Use **Select by expression** to select features, e.g. `\"fclass\" = 'residential'`.\n\n(@) Save selected features to a new layer (**Layer → Save As** or **Export → Save Selected Features As**). Name and store methodically.\n\n(@) Repeat for other relevant landuse classes.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Taking stock of planning constraints\n\n(@) Review the layers that represent areas unsuitable for wind farm development because of their position within, or proximity to, man‑made or natural features.\n\n(@) Explore unconstrained areas large enough for a wind farm. Use subsequent exercises to evaluate the best site.\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n# Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this exercise you should:\n\n1. Calculate area and perimeter of a polygon using the Field Calculator.\n2. Clip point, line, and polygon vector layers.\n3. Create and dissolve buffers around point, line, and polygon layers.\n4. Select and save subsets from a vector layer by attribute.\n5. Build layers that represent your tile’s wind farm planning constraints.\n:::\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":n